//? Title: Bitonic Sort: A Fixed Network with Visible Comparators
//? Subtitle: Four-wire execution, exact size and depth, arbitrary lengths and the zero-one principle
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given an unordered sequence, sort it using comparison locations determined only
by its length. A fixed network exposes independent comparison stages, while the
values decide which exchanges occur. We inspect a four-wire execution, retain
the original eighteen-element example, derive exact power-of-two comparison
counts, and exhaust binary inputs to check the zero-one principle. The sorter,
network inspector and execution trace share one canonical comparator traversal.
)

\toc

> The method

A compare/exchange operation sends the smaller and larger of two values to
specified wires. A bitonic sorter recursively sorts opposing halves, then
merges the resulting bitonic sequence. For power-of-two length n, the merge
first compares wires separated by n/2 and then merges the two halves.

For zero-one input, a cyclic bitonic sequence has one contiguous block of ones
on a circle. Comparing opposite positions separates the smaller from the larger
half, leaves each half bitonic, and ensures every value in the first half is at
most every value in the second. Recursing establishes the merge; recursively
forming the opposing sorted halves establishes the sorter.

The wire pairs and directions depend only on n. They form a sorting network,
and comparisons in a dependency stage use disjoint wires. The native C++ sorter
executes them sequentially; a stage label is an available parallel schedule,
not a measurement of threads or GPU execution.

> Simple example: four wires

Sort \((3,1,4,2)\). The first stage sorts the left pair downward and the right
pair upward. Two upward merge stages then finish the sequence. The diagram
and event table come from the actual core topology and integer trace.

```{cpp, file="figures/bitonic_network.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::bitonic_sort_trace({3,1,4,2});
  const auto network=dm::algorithms::bitonic_network(4);
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(75,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke wire;wire.color={148,163,184};wire.width=1;
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<4;++i) {
    const double y=-1.25*i;
    diagram.path({{0,y},{8,y}},wire)
           .text({-.7,y},std::to_string(trace.input[i]))
           .text({8.7,y},std::to_string(trace.output[i]));
  }
  std::vector<int> within(network.stages);
  for (const auto& c:network.comparators) {
    const double x=1.5+2.5*c.stage+.35*(within[c.stage]++-.5);
    const double first=-1.25*c.first,second=-1.25*c.second;
    dm::graphics::DiagramStroke stroke;
    stroke.color=c.ascending?dm::RGB{37,99,235}:dm::RGB{217,119,6};
    diagram.path({{x,first},{x,second}},stroke)
           .ellipse({x,first},.065,.065,stroke.color)
           .ellipse({x,second},.065,.065,stroke.color);
  }
  for (std::size_t stage=0;stage<network.stages;++stage)
    diagram.text({1.5+2.5*stage,1},std::format("Stage {}",stage+1));
  diagram.text({4,-5},"Blue: minimum to upper wire; amber: minimum to lower wire.");
  diagram.save_svg("build/bitonic_network.svg");
}
```
