\caption(The original non-power-of-two sorting example is retained. Unchanged pairs still count as comparisons. Trace order is sequential recursion order, with stage labels identifying independent groups.)

> Arbitrary lengths need the right merge invariant

The implementation follows Lang's generalisation. Let s be the greatest power
of two strictly below n. Compare \((i,i+s)\) only while both wires exist,
then recurse on lengths s and n-s. Ascending sorting first builds a binary
valley, \(1^*0^*1^*\), from a descending left part and ascending right part.
Padding that valley with ones preserves its shape; comparisons against the
padded ones do nothing. The power-of-two merge therefore restricts to the
real wires, leaving a power-of-two bitonic left part and a valley-shaped right
part for recursion. Descending sorting has the symmetric zero-padding argument.
This is not a claim that every arbitrary-length cyclic bitonic input can be
merged by this restricted operation.

> Checking the theory: exact counts

For \(n=2^p\), a merge has p layers and n/2 comparisons per layer. If M is
merge work and C is sorting work,

$$
M(n)=\frac n2\log_2n,\qquad
C(n)=2C(n/2)+M(n)=\frac n4p(p+1),\qquad
D(n)=\frac{p(p+1)}2.
$$
\alttext(A merge uses n over two times log base two of n comparisons. Sorting uses n over four times p times p plus one comparisons, arranged in p times p plus one over two stages.)

The formula follows by summing the merge work at each recursive level. The
leading term \(np^2/4\) omits \(np/4\); it is not the exact count. For
arbitrary n, the emitted network gives the actual comparison count. Its depth
is \(q(q+1)/2\) for \(q=\lceil\log_2n\rceil\), with zero depth for
n=0 or n=1, and at most \(\lfloor n/2\rfloor\) comparisons per stage.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bitonic_counts.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <bit>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double> size,actual,leading,power_size,predicted;
  std::print("| power-of-two n | emitted comparators | exact formula | stages |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t n=2;n<=64;++n) {
    const auto network=dm::algorithms::bitonic_network(n);
    size.push_back(n);actual.push_back(network.comparators.size());
    const double p=std::log2(double(n));leading.push_back(n*p*p/4);
    if (std::has_single_bit(n)) {
      const auto exponent=std::bit_width(n)-1;
      const auto count=(n/2)*(exponent*(exponent+1)/2);
      if (count!=network.comparators.size()) return 1;
      std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",n,network.comparators.size(),count,network.stages);
      power_size.push_back(n);predicted.push_back(count);
    }
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(size,actual,"network count",dm::RGB{37,99,235},2)
      .lines(size,leading,"leading term",dm::RGB{148,163,184},2)
      .points(power_size,predicted,"power-of-two exact",dm::RGB{217,119,6},6)
      .title("Fixed comparator counts by input length")
      .x_label("number of elements").y_label("comparators").y_limits(0,900)
      .size(900,480).save_svg("build/bitonic_counts.svg");
}
```
