\alttext(The comparator count grows with input length. At lengths two, four, eight, sixteen, thirty-two and sixty-four it is one, six, twenty-four, eighty, two hundred forty and six hundred seventy-two.)

> The zero-one principle and value-dependent swaps

Thresholding commutes with min and max. If a comparison network left an inversion
between two ordered values, a threshold between them would leave an inverted
binary pair. Hence a network that sorts every binary input of length n sorts
every totally ordered input of that length. The following exhaustive check
uses that principle for small lengths, rather than assuming random tests alone
prove correctness.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::print("| wires | binary inputs checked | failures |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (unsigned n=0;n<=10;++n) {
    unsigned failures=0;
    for (unsigned mask=0;mask<(1U<<n);++mask) {
      std::vector<int> values(n);
      for (unsigned j=0;j<n;++j) values[j]=(mask>>j)&1;
      dm::algorithms::bitonic_sort(values);
      failures+=!std::is_sorted(values.begin(),values.end());
    }
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n",n,1U<<n,failures);
    if (failures) return 1;
  }
  std::print("\n| input class | comparisons | exchanges |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& [label,values]:std::vector<std::pair<std::string,std::vector<int>>>{
       {"ascending",{0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7}},{"descending",{7,6,5,4,3,2,1,0}},{"equal",{1,1,1,1,1,1,1,1}}}) {
    const auto trace=dm::algorithms::bitonic_sort_trace(values);
    const auto swaps=std::count_if(trace.steps.begin(),trace.steps.end(),[](const auto& step){return step.swapped;});
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n",label,trace.steps.size(),swaps);
  }
}
```
