// result_end
\caption(Every displayed length is checked on all its binary inputs. Equal-size inputs keep the comparator topology but can cause different numbers of exchanges.)

Native tests extend the binary check through sixteen wires: 131,071 input
sequences in total. They also replay each trace, verify that stage wire pairs
are disjoint, and compare generated integer and floating sequences with ordinary
sorting. Finite exhaustive checks validate those sizes; the recursive argument
establishes the construction's general correctness.

> Conclusion

One canonical visitor now supplies sorting, comparator counts, dependency stages
and recorded compare/exchange events. The original eighteen-element example
sorts without padding the caller's vector. Exact counts and exhaustive binary
controls support the network description, with generated inputs sufficient for
these ordering properties.

The native implementation remains sequential and uses branches and exchanges
that depend on values. Fixed comparator locations do not establish constant-time
execution. The sort is not stable; descending comparisons retain the historical
exchange-on-equality rule. The unrecorded sorter uses a logarithmic recursion
stack, while materialising a topology or trace adds storage proportional to
the number of comparisons. No observed dataset or runtime speed claim is needed.

> References

- Batcher, K. E. (1968). *Sorting networks and their applications*. AFIPS Spring Joint Computer Conference, 32, 307–314. https://doi.org/10.1145/1468075.1468121
- Lang, H. W. *Bitonic sorting network for n not a power of 2*. The arbitrary-length construction and binary padding proof are followed here. https://hwlang.de/algorithmen/sortieren/bitonic/oddn.htm
